-- §7  Which buffers the corpus actually sizes, and the one that reads zero.
# One row per (corpus layer, buffer): whether that buffer was sized, and the typed absence if not.
# A bound is worth exactly as much as the rows it has to bound, so this is the coverage for the
# two inequalities built on `S`.
#
# The two inequalities
#   `Σ_{j ≠ unrealised} H[l,j] ≤ S[l, A(l)]` bounds the filed shares against the slack of the
#   buffer the remainder names as its absorber, and the `capacity` rows close a second inequality
#   on top of that, the one bounding what a filing's own demand and nameplate say could have gone
#   unserved.
#
# The interesting answer reads zero
#   And there are two ways to read zero. Every corpus layer that sizes its capacity slack sizes it
#   at [0,0,0], a filer saying the supply cannot be run above its rating at any price. That is the
#   tightest possible bound rather than a missing one, so the shortfall inequality is exercised,
#   not silent.
#
# Prose about this column goes stale
#   A sentence like "no layer in this corpus sizes its capacity slack" is true until a spelling
#   moves, and this one moves: turn the `absent/reason = none` slacks into claimed zeros and the
#   column fills while any prose beside it stands still, because nothing printed the number.
#   examples/observations/main.rs prints it. A bound with nothing to bound passes loudest, so the
#   trap is real; it is just not this column.
#
# A NULL `absent` is not `unmeasured`
#   A NULL `absent` beside an unsized row is not the same as `unmeasured`. The schema's CHECK
#   constraints decide which combinations are legal; this query only reports what is there.
-- entries/slacks.sqlc at corpus scope.
SELECT s.filing            AS "filing!",
       s.layer             AS "layer!",
       s.buffer::text      AS "buffer!",
       s.sized             AS "sized!",
       s.absent::text      AS "absent"
FROM (
    :compose(entries/slacks.sqlc)
) s
JOIN (
    :compose(scope/corpus.sqlc)
) f USING (filing)
ORDER BY s.filing, s.layer, s.buffer
